<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec13" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

    <title>Basis</title>
    <p>
        Throughout this investigation, <m>V</m> will denote a <em>finite dimensional</em> vector spaces of dimension <m>n</m> over the field <m>F</m>.
        You will analyze the notion of a basis.
        You will prove results to help you identify bases and you will verify one of the core properties of bases.
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-basis">
        <title>Basis</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\mathcal{B}\subset V</m> is an independent, spanning set, then <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a <term>basis</term> for <m>V</m>.
                If we fix an order in which we write the vectors, we call it an <term>ordered basis</term>.
                The plural of basis is <term>bases</term>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        By <xref ref="cor_dimension_well_defined"/>, all bases have the same cardinality and the cardinality of a basis is the dimension.
    </p>
    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is an <m>n</m>-dimensional vector space and <m>S\subset V</m> is a spanning set with <m>n</m> elements, then <m>S</m> is a basis.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a proof by contradiction.
            Suppose <m>S</m> is not a basis.
            Then <m>S</m> is dependent (why?).
            Deduce <m>\dim V&lt;n</m>, yielding a contradiction.
            You may find <xref ref="def-basis"/>, <xref ref="thm-rra"/>, and <xref ref="def-dimension"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-basis2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is an <m>n</m>-dimensional vector space and <m>S\subset V</m> is an independent set with <m>n</m> elements, then <m>S</m> is a basis.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a proof by contradiction.
            Suppose <m>S</m> is not a basis.
            Then <m>S</m> does not span <m>V</m> (why?).
            Deduce <m>\dim V&gt;n</m>, yielding a contradiction.
            You may find <xref ref="def-basis"/>, <xref ref="prop-dependence-prop5"/>, and <xref ref="def-dimension"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-basis-lincomb_exists_unique">
        <title>Every vector can be written uniquely as a linear combination of basis vectors</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\mathcal{B}= \{\vect{v}_1,\ldots , \vect{v}_n\} \subset V</m> is an ordered basis and <m>\vect{w}\in V</m>, then there exists a unique collection of scalars <m>x_1,\ldots, x_n\in F</m> such that <m>\vect{w}=x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            Existence follows immediately from the definition (why???).
            To show uniqueness, suppose there exists <m>x_1,\ldots x_n\in F</m> and <m>y_1,\ldots y_n\in F</m> such that <m>\vect{w}=x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n</m> and <m>\vect{w}=y_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +y_n\vect{v}_n</m>.
            Add the additive inverse of the second expression to the first.
            Deduce <m>x_1=y_1</m>, <m>\ldots</m> <m>x_n=y_n</m> (how???).
            You may find <xref ref="def-basis"/>, <xref ref="def-spanning"/>, <xref ref="def-dependence"/>, and <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <insight>
    <p>
        As we shall see in future sections, bases are absolutely central to the study of linear algebra.
        A basis contains the essence of the vector space it spans.
        Think about what you just proved in <xref ref="prop-basis-lincomb_exists_unique"/> and explain how a basis <q>contains the essence of the vector space it spans.</q>
    </p>
    </insight>

</section>








